According to its graph, the one-sided limits at $x=3$ are different for both functions and therefore $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 3}h(x)$ and $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 3}g(x)$ don't exist.

However, before we jump to any conclusions regarding $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 3}\Big(h(x)g(x)\Big)$, let's consider the one-sided limits:

$\begin{align}
&\blueD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 3^-}h(x)=1}
\\\\
&\purpleD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 3^+}h(x)=5}
\\\\
&\maroonD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 3^-}g(x)=5}
\\\\
&\goldD{\lim_{x\to 3^+}g(x)=1}
\end{align}$

We can find each one-sided limit of $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 3}\Big(h(x)g(x)\Big)$ using the fact that the limit of a product of functions is the product of the individual limits:

$\begin{align}
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 3^-}\Big(h(x)g(x)\Big)=\lim_{x\to 3^-}h(x)\cdot \lim_{x\to 3^-}g(x)
\\\\
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 3^+}\Big(h(x)g(x)\Big)=\lim_{x\to 3^+}h(x)\cdot \lim_{x\to 3^+}g(x)
\end{align}$
